Frozen Shoulder (Adhesive Capsulitis)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Global restriction of active and passive motion, Profound loss of passive external rotation, Insidious aching shoulder pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-024
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe nocturnal shoulder pain causing complete sleep deprivation and functional distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing a severe, unrelenting, deep aching pain in the shoulder that prevents sleep and strictly limits movement. Urgent assessment was required to definitively differentiate a benign but severely painful "freezing" phase of adhesive capsulitis from an acute, catastrophic full-thickness rotator cuff tear or septic joint, and to provide potent acute analgesia to restore basic sleep capability before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My shoulder is locked in a vise of pain; it throbs relentlessly at night, and if I accidentally reach for something, the sharp pain drops me to my knees."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with severe exacerbation of right shoulder pain in the context of an insidious, 2-month history of worsening stiffness. Timeline: Gradual onset of a deep, dull, aching discomfort localized over the deltoid insertion; severely worsened over the last two weeks, marking the transition into the highly painful "freezing" phase. Patient reports profound disruption of sleep, unable to lie on the affected side. Today, the patient experienced a sudden, agonizing sharp pain when reaching blindly into the back seat of a car, rendering the arm completely immobile. Difficulty performing any overhead activities or reaching behind the back to dress.
Functional Status: Significant functional impairment; unable to use the right arm for reaching, lifting, or grooming.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation compounding a high level of functional distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, visibly fatigued, cradling the right arm across the abdomen.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals compensatory excessive scapulothoracic shrugging when the patient attempts to elevate the arm. Crucial finding: There is a severe, global restriction of both active and passive shoulder range of motion. Forward flexion is limited to 70 degrees. Hallmark finding: Profound loss of passive external rotation (<10 degrees) with the arm resting comfortably at the side, limited by a firm, incredibly painful mechanical block. Absence of focal point tenderness over the bicipital groove or specific rotator cuff insertions. No erythema or joint effusion. Pulses and distal sensation intact.
Environmental Observations: Patient sleeping in a recliner chair due to inability to lie flat in bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular paracetamol with negligible effect on the severe capsular pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Adhesive Capsulitis (Frozen Shoulder) - Active "Freezing" Phase - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Acute Rotator Cuff Tear - Ruled out clinically by the profound, equal loss of both active AND passive range of motion. (A cuff tear typically preserves passive motion while active motion is lost).

Glenohumeral Osteoarthritis - Unlikely as a primary acute cause given the lack of bony crepitus during the limited arc of motion, though requires formal radiographic exclusion.

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out by the insidious, months-long onset, absence of systemic fever, and lack of severe, tracking erythema or generalized systemic toxicity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in the excruciating "freezing" phase of adhesive capsulitis, characterized by profound loss of passive external rotation and severe nocturnal pain. The critical clinical distinction was differentiating this globally stiff joint from a structurally torn rotator cuff or a surgical emergency. Clinical examination definitively established a mechanical capsular block (loss of passive motion) without signs of infection or acute massive structural failure. The severity of the pain, resulting in total sleep deprivation and functional loss, created an acute distress state. Urgent after-hours assessment provided crucial diagnostic clarity, prevented inappropriate forceful manipulation by the patient, and allowed for the initiation of targeted anti-inflammatory strategies to manage the severe capsular pain.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 7 days to aggressively target the capsular inflammation. Provided a short 3-day course of Codeine 30mg PRN strictly for nocturnal use to allow sleep.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use a thick pillow to support the elbow and forearm when sitting or sleeping, keeping the arm slightly away from the body. Consequence: Prevents the inflamed capsule from being stretched tightly across the joint, leading to a rapid decrease in the constant, deep throbbing ache (negative reinforcement).
Action: Perform slow, gravity-assisted "pendulum" swings with the arm while leaning forward, strictly within the pain-free zone. Consequence: Gentle mechanical lubrication of the joint space, preventing the capsule from further scarring and locking down tighter overnight (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7-10 days for consideration of an intra-articular corticosteroid injection, which is highly effective in the painful freezing phase, and subsequent physiotherapy referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the shoulder suddenly becomes intensely hot, red, and swollen, if a high fever develops, or if there is new numbness shooting down into the hand.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of joint stiffness. Formal imaging (ultrasound/x-ray) is usually arranged by the regular GP to rule out underlying calcification or arthritis. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Frozen shoulder has three phases: Freezing (very painful), Frozen (stiff but less painful), and Thawing (improving mobility). You are in the highly painful first phase. Total recovery can take 12-24 months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you develop a fever and the joint becomes red and hot, or if you suddenly lose feeling in your arm or hand.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The capsule surrounding your shoulder joint has become severely inflamed and is shrinking like tight shrink-wrap around the bone. This causes intense pain when stretched. The anti-inflammatories will reduce the swelling inside the capsule, while the nighttime painkiller will allow you to sleep. Do not force the arm past the point of sharp pain, as this will only tear the inflamed tissue and make it tighter.
Actionable Steps: Prop the arm on a pillow tonight to stop it from hanging and stretching the capsule. Do the gentle pendulum swings to keep the fluid moving inside the joint, but never push into the sharp, catching pain.

